result_text:
The `amount` values are strings (e.g. `"42.10"`), so `+` concatenates instead of adding. Starting from `0`, you get `"0" + "42.10"` → `"042.10"`, then `"042.10" + "15.00"` → `"042.1015.00"`, and so on. That matches your output.

Convert each amount to a number before summing:

```js
// Expenses page: the list and the total.
function total(items) {
  return items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + Number(item.amount), 0);
}
module.exports = { total };
```

Floating-point addition can produce results like `0.1 + 0.2 = 0.30000000000000004`. If you display the total directly, format it at the display site with `total(items).toFixed(2)`. Alternatively, sum in cents inside `total`:

```js
function total(items) {
  const cents = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + Math.round(Number(item.amount) * 100), 0);
  return cents / 100;
}
```

If any amount can be empty or non-numeric, `Number(...)` returns `NaN` and the total becomes `NaN`. In that case, you'd want to validate the data or skip those items. The better long-term fix is to parse amounts into numbers where the data is loaded, so the rest of the code doesn't have to care.

I can't see the rest of your code, so I haven't verified where the strings come from.

